(footprint "XL-3030RGBC-WS2812B" (version 20221018) (generator pcbnew)
  (layer "F.Cu")
  (descr "XINGLIGHT XL-3030RGBC-WS2812B / LCSC C5349958. Addressable RGB LED, 3.0 x 3.0 mm, 4 corner pads. TOE EXTENDED +0.35 mm PER PAD, a DELIBERATE departure from the datasheet Recommended Soldering Pattern (0.9 x 0.8 at 2.5 / 2.0 c/c, which this footprint matched exactly before). Pads are now 1.25 x 0.8 at (+/-1.425, +/-1.0): the HEEL is unchanged at x +/-0.80, so nothing under the package moves - only the outer edge, +/-1.70 -> +/-2.05, taking the overhang past the 3.0 mm body from 0.20 to 0.55 mm. WHY: with a 0.20 mm toe the joint sits under the package and forms no inspectable fillet, so optical inspection cannot distinguish a good joint from a cold one or a head-in-pillow - which left a v3.2 LED-chain field failure (2 of 10 halves) undiagnosable. 0.70 mm gives AOI and a loupe a visible meniscus, and gives a fine soldering-iron tip somewhere to land for rework without going under the part. PASTE FOLLOWS THE COPPER 1:1 (no margin). That is deliberate: what matters is solder THICKNESS, not total volume. Copper grows 0.72 -> 1.00 mm2 (+39%), so paste must grow with it to hold ~0.050 mm3 of solder per mm2 of copper - the same as the current 0.9 x 0.8 joint. Insetting the paste instead (e.g. margin -0.05 -> 1.20 x 0.70) spreads roughly the same solder over 39% more copper and yields a joint THINNER than today's, defeating the fillet this change exists to create. The extra paste does not go under the package: the heel is fixed at x +/-0.80, so paste over the terminal is unchanged and the addition sits over bare toe copper, which is where the fillet forms. WHY NOT MORE TOE: the fillet meniscus forms within ~0.2-0.4 mm of the terminal edge, so inspection value saturates by ~0.5 mm; and a 1.4 mm pad with paste following 1:1 measured +56% solder, enough to float or tilt this light package. TOMBSTONING IS NOT A MECHANISM HERE: drawbridging needs a two-terminal part able to rotate about one end; four symmetric corner pads constrain both axes and the equal extension preserves self-centering. PIN-1 MARKER: a filled 0.40 mm dot on F.SilkS and F.Fab beside pad 1 (top-left), centred at (-2.5, -1.3), 0.25 mm clear of the pad edge. The pre-existing filled triangle at the BOTTOM-RIGHT (beside pad 3) is DELIBERATELY RETAINED - do not delete it: LCSC/EasyEDA's own reference footprint places its triangle at the same corner, so it marks a real feature of the package, not pin 1. The two marks say different things: triangle = package corner, dot = pin 1. The triangle alone was ambiguous enough that the assembler repeatedly asked which pin is 1, which is why the dot was added rather than the triangle moved. Y IS UNCHANGED (0.8 tall, +/-1.0), so the 0.710 mm clearance to the Kailh socket pad above each LED is untouched; the courtyard grew in x only, +/-1.5 -> +/-2.25 - it bounds the BODY AND PADS (outer edge +/-2.05) plus a 0.20 mm margin, NOT the silkscreen. The pin-1 dot deliberately sits outside it, which is normal: a courtyard is a placement/collision envelope for the physical part, and sizing it to enclose a silk marker made it 0.80 mm too wide on each side. Pin numbering unchanged: 1 VDD, 2 DOUT, 3 VSS, 4 DIN.")
